    <exercise number="59" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>What is the domain of the function $$f(x) = \ln\left( \frac{x + 2}{x - 4} \right)?$$ Discuss the result.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>Recall that the argument of a logarithmic function must be positive, so we determine where $$x+2 x-4 &gt;0 x+2 x-4 &gt;0$$ . From the graph of the function $$f( x )= x+2 x-4 , f( x )= x+2 x-4 ,$$ note that the graph lies above the x-axis on the interval $$( -\infty ,-2 ) ( -\infty ,-2 )$$ and again to the right of the vertical asymptote, that is $$( 4,\infty ). ( 4,\infty ).$$ Therefore, the domain is $$( -\infty ,-2 )\cup ( 4,\infty ). ( -\infty ,-2 )\cup ( 4,\infty ).$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
